WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.360
Security includes the ability to recover.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:06.660
A Linux system can be well configured and still suffer from hardware failure,

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:12.320
accidental deletion, bad updates, ransomware, operator mistakes, failed deployments,

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.660
or damaged

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.000
configuration.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.700
Backups reduce the impact of those events.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.080
They protect data, configuration, and sometimes whole system states,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.540
depending on the recovery needs of the environment.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.720
A backup is only useful if it can be restored.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.380
Restore testing proves that the backup contains the right data,

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.160
that procedures are understood, that permissions and ownership can be recovered correctly,

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:37.340
and that

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.720
recovery time is realistic.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.180
Teams should know what is backed up, where it is stored, how long it is retained,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.360
who can access it, and what would happen

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.740
if the primary system were unavailable.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.280
Change control protects availability.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.580
It is not paperwork for its own sake.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.680
It gives administrators a way to plan risky work, communicate timing, check dependencies,

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.460
confirm backups, document expected impact, and define rollback steps.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.600
Hardening changes can be valuable,

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.020
but they can also break applications when assumptions are wrong.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.920
A change window and rollback plan make security work safer.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.220
Exceptions should also be tracked.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.280
A system might miss a patch because an application vendor has not certified it,

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.540
or a firewall rule might remain open for a temporary

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:24.720
migration.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:29.340
Those decisions should have owners, expiration dates, and validation.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.760
Recovery readiness ties the course together: least privilege, patching, monitoring,

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.820
and hardening all matter more when the team can reverse mistakes and restore service.
